sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch07-04> a delphi:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Hardware noise-based random generators deviate from perfect randomness" ;
    dct:description "Devices that generate randomness by amplifying microscopic environmental noise (spark gaps, vacuum tubes, semiconductor diodes) have consistently been found to produce output with significant deviations from perfect randomness, because the sampling device relaxes only gradually and because the environment itself has correlations." ;
    delphi:context "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 7, “Mechanisms in Programs and Nature”; section “Randomness from the Environment” (from p. 301), p. 303. Scope: physical random number generators based on electronic or other environmental noise" ;
    dct:type "model-of-nature" .
}

